\composer[c.\,1524 – c.\,1560]{Wacław z Szamotuł}
\title{Nunc scio vere}
\subtitle{Introitus in festo SS. Apostolorum Petri et Pauli}
\edition{First edition}{2026}

\begin{document}
\makemasthead
\lilypondfile[noindent]{music/score-performance.ly}

\section*{Text and translation}

\textandtranslation

\noindent Acts 12:11; Psalm 138 (139):1–2.

\begin{rehearsal}
\pnote{The work} The setting survives only in a textless organ tablature of
about 1590. It is built on the Introit chant, which passes between the
voices (marked [c.f.]). The words are editorial. The critical edition gives
the sources and the reasons for each reading.

\pnote{Order} Antiphon (choir). Psalm verse (cantor, in chant; the choir may
join at the asterisk). \la{Gloria Patri} (choir). Antiphon repeated. The
organ may take the \la{Gloria Patri} or the repeat, as it often did in
Wacław’s time, while the choir says the words quietly.

\pnote{Pitch and tempo} The score is at written pitch, for men’s voices
with boys or falsettists on the Cantus. A mixed choir should sing a minor
third higher, where the ranges match Wacław’s other motets. Minim pulse
about 60–72. The organ may double the voices.

\pnote{The words} Polish Latin, as Wacław’s singers spoke it; the series
pronunciation guide gives the sounds. Stress: \la{Dóminus, ángelum,
erípuit, Heródis, exspectatióne, Iudaeórum, princípio, saeculórum}.
\textit{Ti} before a vowel is \textit{tsi}, its \textit{i} a syllable of
its own, as the underlay has it: ex-spe-cta-ti-o-ne.

\pnote{Signs} Accidentals before notes come from the source and apply to
their own note only. Accidentals above the staff are editorial; a bracketed one is optional. Italic
words are repetitions of text. The small note in the Altus at bar 56 is
editorial; the tied c′ before it may be held instead.
\end{rehearsal}
\footcolophon
